

# Arduino Google TV IP Remote

An ESP32-S3 project for discovering Google TV and Android TV devices on the
local network and, eventually, controlling them through the Google TV Remote
Control v2 protocol.

The project is inspired by:

- [ESP32 Android TV Remote v2](https://github.com/nguyluky/ESP32_android_tv_remote_v2)
- [Google TV (aka Android TV) Remote Control (v2) wiki](https://github.com/Aymkdn/assistant-freebox-cloud/wiki/Google-TV-(aka-Android-TV)-Remote-Control-(v2))

The protocol uses Protocol Buffers. This project uses
[nanopb](https://github.com/nanopb/nanopb) to serialize and deserialize the
Google pairing and remote protocol messages on the ESP32. The protocol schema
files are in `src/GoogleNanoPB_Remote_protocol/`.

## Current functionality

- Connects the ESP32 to Wi-Fi through WiFiManager.
- Discovers `_androidtvremote2._tcp` services with mDNS.
- Reads additional device information from `_googlecast._tcp` TXT records.
- Resolves the discovered device IP address to a network MAC address when
  possible.
- Generates nanopb sources from the configured protocol schema during the
	pioarduino build.

Pairing, encrypted communication, and key input transmission are represented
by the protocol definitions but are still being integrated into the remote
class.

## Hardware and software

- ESP32-S3 with 16 MB flash and 8 MB PSRAM
- VS Code with pioarduino
- A Google TV or Android TV on the same Wi-Fi network
- ESP-IDF managed component support

## Compile directions

1. Make sure `src/idf_component.yml` is present and contains the
	`espressif/mdns` and `Wolfssl` dependency.

  ```text
  dependencies:
  idf: ">=5.0"
  espressif/mdns:   ">1.8.2"
  wolfssl/wolfssl:  ">5.8.2"
  
  
```
2. Run the pioarduino menuconfig target to download and configure the ESP-IDF
	managed components:

	```text
	pio run -t menuconfig
	```

3. Update the `mDNS` and `Wolfssl` and include path in `platformio.ini` to match the project
	location on your computer. For example:

	```ini
	lib_ldf_mode = off
	lib_compat_mode = strict
	build_flags = 
		-D WM_NOHELP						;do not show wifi manager help on the info page
		-D WOLFSSL_USER_SETTINGS			; use the user settigns file in the lib
		-D WOLFSSL_ESP32
		-I D:/Documents/Arduino_GoogleTV_IP_Remote/include 
		-I D:/Documents/Arduino_GoogleTV_IP_Remote/managed_components/espressif__mdns/include/
		-I D:/Documents/Arduino_GoogleTV_IP_Remote/managed_components/wolfssl__wolfssl/include/

	build_src_filter =
  		-<../managed_components/wolfssl__wolfssl/include/user_settings.h>
	```

4. Rename the file in D:/Documents/Arduino_GoogleTV_IP_Remote/managed_component/wolfssl__wolfssl/include/user_settings.h to user_setting.h

5. Build the project:

	```text
	pio run
	```

6. Upload it to the board and open the serial monitor at 115200 baud:

	```text
	pio run -t upload
	pio device monitor -b 115200
	```

The default environment is `esp32s3-n16r8-USBOTG`. Nanopb is declared in
`platformio.ini` as `nanopb/Nanopb`, currently pinned to the `0.4.x` release
line. The build configuration generates sources under pioarduino's build
directory from the `.proto` files selected by `custom_nanopb_protos`.

## Project layout

```text
src/
  GoogleIP_Remote.cpp       mDNS discovery and network helpers
  GoogleIP_Remote.h         Google TV remote interface and device model
  main.cpp                  ESP32 application entry point
  GoogleNanoPB_Remote_protocol/
	 *.proto                 Google TV pairing and remote protocol schemas
```

